    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Troy began as Columbia, a settlement mapped by James Riggen, before settlers renamed it Troy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://troyil.us/194/History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was incorporated on February 18, 1857, and later achieved city status in 1892.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Troy%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1993, eight enthusiasts met at Randy’s Restaurant to launch the Troy Historical Society, now a community education hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.troyhistoricalsociety.org/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The society’s gatherings have shifted from the old library building to the new library’s community room, and once to the Wiesemeyer Center basement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        13,
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.troyhistoricalsociety.org/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Early Troy’s economy revolved around a band mill, storehouse, taverns, and housing, reflecting its modest 19th‑century roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://troyil.us/194/History",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Illinois Route 162 slices through Troy’s core as Center Street, Market Street, and Edwardsville Road, linking to US 40 and Maryville.",
